%[text] # Nise 2.13
%[text] Enter the following transfer into MATLAB
%[text] $G(s) = \\frac{5(s+15)(s+26)(s+72)}{s(s+55)(s^2+5s+30)(s+56)(s^2+27s+52)}$
%[text] ## Ratio of Factors (zpk) Method
%[text] First, use the roots() function to find the poles associate with the two quadratic terms int he denominator.
a= roots([1 5 30]) %[output:77562e8c]
b= roots([1 27 52]) %[output:67346deb]
%[text] Note the that results are 2x1 **column vectors**.
%[text] Collect the real valued poles as a vector.
real_poles = [0, -55, -56] %[output:1d223a7c]
%[text] Note that this generates a 1x3 **row vector**.
%[text] How to we concatenate the two three vectors?  We could combine them as a set of column vectors,
poles_column = [a; %[output:group:9b11b3dc] %[output:4ac1d7f0]
                b;  %[output:4ac1d7f0]
                real_poles'] %[output:group:9b11b3dc] %[output:4ac1d7f0]
%[text] Or we could concatenate the three vectors as row vectors,
poles_row = [a', b', real_poles] %[output:734170c5]
%[text] Either way can be used with zpk
zpk_column= zpk([-15 -26 -72],poles_column,5) %[output:64b1d533]
zpk_row= zpk([-15 -26 -72],poles_row,5) %[output:260742d9]

